      .toList();

  ═══ EFFECTIVELY FINAL CAPTURE ═══

  BigDecimal threshold = new BigDecimal("100000");  // effectively final (Recommended) 
  // threshold = BigDecimal.ZERO;                   // COMPILE ERROR — breaks effectively final!

  Lambda captures the VALUE of threshold (a copy).
  If threshold could change after capture, the lambda would have a stale copy → bug.
  Java prevents this by requiring effectively final.

  Workaround: AtomicInteger counter (reference is final, internal value is mutable)
  counter.incrementAndGet() inside lambda → OK because counter reference never changes.

  ═══ LAMBDA vs ANONYMOUS CLASS — UNDER THE HOOD ═══

  Anonymous class:
    → generates Trade$1.class file at compile time
    → instantiates a new object on every use
    → `this` inside refers to the anonymous class

  Lambda:
    → uses invokedynamic bytecode (no .class file!)
    → JVM creates lightweight impl at runtime (method handle)
    → `this` inside refers to the ENCLOSING class (not the lambda)
    → faster instantiation, less memory
